	title = {\enquote{{{Now I Can Go On}}!}: {{The Collapse}} of {{Linguistic Authority}} in {{Beckett}}'s {{Endgame}}},
	year = {2022},
	abstract = {This article argues that Stanley Cavell, in his reading of Beckett's Endgame, overlooks a crucial dimension of the play's language, and it supports this claim by engaging with Cavell's own philosophy. Cavell proposes that Endgame's characters go on using words in familiar ways. But the play also explores how words assume new meanings under radically altered circumstances. This possibility of a word's old meaning being \enquote{projected} into new contexts is one Cavell discusses in The Claim of Reason, and the conclusions he draws (via his distinctive reading of Wittgenstein) help to clarify the concerns of Beckett's play.},
