	abstract = {The purpose of this paper is to consider how we should interpret the evidence concerning naval developments in the archaic period. Ideally we might hope to be able to achieve some sort of `marriage' of the (contemporary) archaeological evidence for archaic ships and harbour installations with the (largely non-contemporary) written evidence for navies and their use. From this combination of sources we might then be able to build up a picture of the development of naval power by the early Greeks.¹ The reality, however, is that such a harmonious union is impossible due to the irreconcilable differences between the various},
