	abstract = {This essay engages themes and core questions marking the readings of Heidegger by some Anglo-American deconstructive thinkers. The first section titled \enquote{Radical finitude, abyssal truth, and radical questioning} sets out with a brief presentation of the threat posed by Derridean deconstruction with respect to more \enquote{orthodox} interpretations of Heidegger's thought and proceeds with an account of Charles Scott's reading of death in Being and Time and then an account of John Sallis' reading of Heidegger's \enquote{On the Essence of Truth.} The second section, titled \enquote{The history of being and transgressing metaphysics,} problematizes Heidegger's thinking of the history of being also with reference to critiques by Anglo-American thinkers. The third section \enquote{Disseminations} joins critiques put forward by Scott, David Farrell Krell, and David Wood with respect to a resistance against dissemination in Heidegger's thought and points to the project of thinking time without a unifying origin.},
